<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Xia (Dou Jiande)</h1><p class="st-meta">617 CE – 621 CE · capital Lezhou</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The rebel kingdom of Dou Jiande on the Hebei plain, remembered as the most humane of the Sui-era insurgent regimes, destroyed at the Battle of Hulao in 621.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dou-jiande">Dou Jiande</a>, a village headman from Zhangnan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>, joined the peasant risings against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang's</a> levies for the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo</a> wars in 611. He took over the band of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-shida">Gao Shida</a>, proclaimed himself Prince of Changle in 617 and Prince of Xia in 618, ruling much of Hebei from Lezhou.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dou-jiande">Dou Jiande</a> won a reputation for frugality, for sparing captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> officials and employing them in his government, and for sharing spoils with his soldiers. In 619 he defeated and executed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-huaji">Yuwen Huaji</a>, the murderer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang</a>, which raised his standing among loyalists of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sui-dynasty">Sui Dynasty</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 621 he marched to relieve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-shichong">Wang Shichong</a>, besieged at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Li Shimin</a> blocked him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hulao">Hulao</a> pass and captured him; he was executed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>. His former officers under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-heita">Liu Heita</a> rose again and held out until 623.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 617 CE – 621 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:71.98%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>617 CE – 621 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lezhou</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Dou Jiande</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>China</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Xia (618-621)</dd></dl></aside></div>
